  "historical_provenance_note": "script_sha256 / tool_sha256 is stamped once, at render time, into the record the script produced. It is NOT re-derived by any test, and superseded values are legitimately committed: as of this registry seven of these eight scripts have drifted from the digests their records carry (current_bytes_recorded: false); fixtures/render_fixture.py is the exception, flipped to true by #126 when the SXT-028f fixture bundles were re-rendered under the pinned oracle. A live-equality assertion on these would still be wrong -- nothing re-derives a script_sha256 stamp, and no entry here carries a sha256 field. Editing one does not invalidate a record; it does mean the record's provenance digest no longer resolves to any file in the tree, so re-rendering is the only way to restore a resolvable stamp. Disposition of the lint findings in these scripts, decided explicitly in #269 (not inherited from #254's source table, one row of which does not hold): they are ordinary code, so a dead-code finding in one is cleanable with no registry ceremony and no leaf re-run -- the per-entry lint_finding_disposition field records that decision for each. For the seven entries that still read false the cost is only that an edit moves the file further from the digests its records carry, and that cost is already spent: those flags were false before #269's cleanup and were re-derived false after it, in the same change. fixtures/render_fixture.py no longer qualifies -- #126 re-rendered its fixtures, so its stamp resolves again and an edit there does spend something; its per-entry disposition records that, and it is still not a live pin. This says nothing about live_pins, where a finding stays permanently kept.",
